[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105578,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Palmar/plantar fibromatoses are benign fibroblastic and myofibroblastic proliferations that involve the volar aspects of the hands or the plantar aponeuroses.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105579,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8813/1 Palmar/plantar fibromatoses</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105580,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FB51.Y &amp; XH75J5 Other specified fibroblastic disorders &amp; Palmar/plantar-type fibromatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105581,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> for palmar fibromatosis: Dupuytren contracture; for plantar fibromatosis: Ledderhose disease.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105582,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105583,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In the paediatric population, palmar fibromatosis arises in the volar aspect of the hand and finger, whereas plantar fibromatosis typically involves the medial aspect of the arch or anteromedial portion of the fat pad of the heel {16006806; 8989694}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105584,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The clinical presentation of palmar fibromatosis includes contractures, swelling, or a painless nodular mass {24163199}. Patients with plantar fibromatosis usually present with asymptomatic nodules {16006806; 8989694}. A subset of patients have synchronous or metachronous lesions {16006806; 1946077}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105585,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although palmar fibromatosis usually affects adults, with incidence increasing with age, rare cases have been reported in children {16006806; 24163199}. Plantar fibromatosis more frequently arises in patients aged &lt;&nbsp;30&nbsp;years, including children as young as 9&nbsp;months {6507097; 16006806; 8989694}. There is no definitive sex predilection in paediatric patients.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105586,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105587,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although superficial fibromatosis lacks somatic <em>CTNNB1</em> or <em>APC</em> mutations, the WNT/&beta;-catenin pathway is probably still involved in the pathogenesis of these tumours {11455002}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105588,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gross examination reveals small (&lt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;mm), poorly defined, rubbery, cord-like nodules with a tan-white cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105589,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Microscopically these tumours are composed of a nodular, sometimes plexiform, arrangement of spindle cells {16006806}. Three phases of growth (proliferative, involutional, and late-stage) have been described, with progression from cellular lesions composed of parallel fascicles of fibroblasts and myofibroblasts to tumours with increased collagen deposition and low cellularity. Mitotic activity can be prominent, but atypical mitoses are absent. Perivascular chronic inflammation, microhaemorrhage, and multinucleated giant cells may be appreciated. Chondroid foci and areas of calcification characteristic of calcifying aponeurotic fibroma are absent.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105590,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105591,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105592,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> long fascicles of bland fibroblasts and myofibroblasts; variably collagenous stroma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105593,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105594,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 152,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>When conservative measures fail, surgical excision is the treatment of choice {29310463}. Recurrence rates are typically high and dependent on the extent of surgical resection.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]